= Inference — Technical =

== Semantic consequence ==
For theory Γ and formula φ:

<pre>
Γ ⊨ φ
</pre>

iff every interpretation satisfying Γ satisfies φ.

This is a model-theoretic relation.

== Syntactic derivation ==
For proof calculus ⊢:

<pre>
Γ ⊢ φ
</pre>

means φ is derivable from Γ by the calculus.

Soundness:

<pre>
Γ ⊢ φ → Γ ⊨ φ
</pre>

Completeness:

<pre>
Γ ⊨ φ → Γ ⊢ φ
</pre>

relative to the logic/calculus.

== Forward chaining ==
Rules:

<pre>
A1 ∧ ... ∧ An → B
</pre>

are repeatedly applied to known facts until no new consequences are produced or a query is answered.

Materialized RDF/RDFS rule engines can use this style.

== Backward chaining ==
Backward chaining begins with a goal and searches for rules whose conclusions could establish it.

This is common in logic programming and query systems.

Termination depends on rule/program structure.

== Resolution ==
Resolution is a proof rule widely used in automated theorem proving.

After conversion to clause form, complementary literals are resolved to derive new clauses.

Refutation proves a goal by deriving contradiction from its negation plus premises.

== Tableau ==
Tableau procedures attempt to construct a model satisfying constraints.

A clash indicates inconsistency along a branch.

Description-logic reasoners commonly use optimized tableau-like methods.

== Probabilistic inference ==
Bayesian networks factor a joint distribution:

<pre>
P(X1,...,Xn) = ∏ P(Xi | Parents(Xi))
</pre>

Inference computes marginals, conditionals or most probable assignments.

Exact inference can be computationally hard; approximate methods include sampling and variational approaches.

== Open-world entailment ==
RDF/OWL entailment generally does not use absence as negation.

Closed-world rule engines may.

Mixing assumptions without explicit boundaries can produce invalid conclusions.

== Inference provenance ==
Derived facts should be traceable to:
* premise assertions;
* ontology/rule version;
* inference engine;
* entailment regime;
* timestamp.

This enables explanation and retraction when premises change.

